WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Filesystem features can complicate path validation.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.680
A path that appears to be inside an approved directory may lead somewhere else through a symbolic link.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:17.660
A mount point may connect an approved-looking location to a different storage area with different trust assumptions.

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.920
Shared writable directories may change between one check and the next.

5
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:28.980
These details matter because path validation is about the actual operation the script will perform, not the visual shape of the path.

6
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.700
Scripts should define what type of object they expect.

7
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:44.080
If the workflow expects a regular input file, a directory, an existing output directory, or a newly created temporary file, that expectation should be checked before sensitive operations.

8
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:54.660
A value that points to a directory when a file was expected, a link when a regular file was expected, or another unexpected filesystem object should not be treated as harmless.

9
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.240
File type is part of validation.

10
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.720
Race-condition awareness matters at a defensive level.

11
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:08.500
The filesystem can change between validation and use, especially in shared writable areas or long-running workflows.

12
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:15.080
A script should keep checks close to the operation they protect and avoid broad trust in mutable locations.

13
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:24.620
Where a workflow is sensitive, prefer controlled directories with restricted write access over shared locations where other users or jobs can change entries unexpectedly.

14
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.980
The goal is not to make every script a filesystem research project.

15
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.200
The goal is to be honest about the features that can change path meaning.

16
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:41.660
If the script's safety depends on a path staying inside a base directory, links, mounts, file types, and shared write access all deserve review.

17
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.280
Good scripts make those assumptions explicit and fail safely when the filesystem does not match the expected shape.
